# ── What was actually read, per protocol ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# ⚠ NuGet v3 — the service index resource types the client resolves, the flat container's lower-cased
# id and normalised version in every path, the multipart body `dotnet nuget push` sends with its
# credential in X-NuGet-ApiKey, and DELETE-as-unlist. Unlisted versions stay in the flat container:
# nuget.org's does the same, and a lock file that pins one keeps restoring. What was NOT read against
# a real client: no `dotnet` process is run by any suite in the tree — the requests are reproduced
# from the client's protocol documentation and the host answers them. conformance.yaml § owed,
# `no-real-client-is-driven`.
#
# ⚠ npm — the publish document with the tarball base64-encoded under `_attachments`, the packument
# with `dist-tags`, `versions` and `time`, `dist.shasum` as SHA-1 and `dist.integrity` as SHA-512,
# scoped names arriving as `@scope%2Fname` on some paths and `@scope/name` on others, and the
# dist-tag routes under `-/package/{name}/dist-tags`. A publish over an existing version answers
# 409; npm's own registry answers 403 for that case with a message, and the CLI reports either.
#
# ⚠ Maven — the repository layout is the protocol. `mvn deploy` GETs maven-metadata.xml, PUTs each
# file with `.sha1` and `.md5` beside it, then PUTs the merged metadata; a resolver reverses it. A
# release path is immutable and a `-SNAPSHOT` version directory is not, which is the default release
# policy of every repository manager. Basic authentication with the platform token as the password,
# because settings.xml has a username and a password and nowhere else to put a token.
#
# ── What is assumed ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# ⚠ THAT THE PLATFORM'S OBJECT STORE IS S3-COMPATIBLE. CyberCloud.ObjectStorage signs with Signature
# Version 4 and speaks four verbs; SeaweedFS accepts all four and validates the signature when an
# identities file is configured, which CyberCloud.ObjectStorage.Tests proves against a container.
# Against an AWS bucket the same four verbs are pinned by Amazon's published vectors and not by a
# request. conformance.yaml § owed, `the-store-is-seaweedfs-in-tests-and-assumed-elsewhere`.

